  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through potential&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>KE</mi><mo>)</mo></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>meV</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math></p><p>where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math>&nbsp;is Planck&#39;s constant,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi></math>&nbsp;is charge of electron&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is mass of electron.</p><p>From above relation,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>V</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>.</p>",
